WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:04.920
DEBI CABRERA: Data by any other
name would still be as sweet,

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:10.200
but how dost thou process
and transform data?

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:13.440
AKA, data is very
powerful, but how do you

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:15.370
process and transform it?

00:00:15.370 --> 00:00:17.820
Join me as I show
you with Apache Beam.

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:21.187
[UPBEAT MUSIC]

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:29.450
In this video, we'll walk
through a demonstration

00:00:29.450 --> 00:00:30.680
of Apache Beam.

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:32.390
If you haven't seen
the last video,

00:00:32.390 --> 00:00:34.580
I recommend checking it out
since we talk about some

00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:37.730
of the key concepts that
make up a pipeline in Beam.

00:00:37.730 --> 00:00:39.900
There's a link in
the description.

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:42.350
In this example, we'll
walk through using

00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:46.070
Beam to count the words from
Shakespeare's "King Lear."

00:00:46.070 --> 00:00:48.590
And we'll show what it looks
like to use Google Cloud

00:00:48.590 --> 00:00:50.840
Dataflow as a runner.

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:54.050
This is a great starting
example of a batch data job,

00:00:54.050 --> 00:00:56.510
since we're working with
a file which is bounded,

00:00:56.510 --> 00:00:59.570
as opposed to streaming data
processing, which is typically

00:00:59.570 --> 00:01:02.930
unbounded because there's
no fixed start or end.

00:01:02.930 --> 00:01:06.380
Let's take a look at what
our batch data pipeline does.

00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:08.510
Here's what the
pipeline looks like.

00:01:08.510 --> 00:01:11.660
The first thing we'll do is read
from a text file that contains

00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:13.910
the entire copy of "King Lear."

00:01:13.910 --> 00:01:16.790
After that, we'll use
transforms to split

00:01:16.790 --> 00:01:19.670
each line into
individual words, count

00:01:19.670 --> 00:01:23.120
the instances of each word,
and output the results.

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:26.660
Each PTransform takes
a PCollection as input

00:01:26.660 --> 00:01:30.050
and sends one out as output
so we can chain together

00:01:30.050 --> 00:01:31.710
the transforms.

00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:33.320
So first things first.

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:35.360
We can check out the input file.

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:38.240
This is just a short
snippet, but the entire book

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:40.950
is included in the text
file we'll be using.

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:43.800
Of course, there's lots
of words in this book.

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:46.650
So let's get started
counting them.

00:01:46.650 --> 00:01:48.800
In this example,
we're using Python.

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:52.970
So the first step is to install
the Apache Beam files we need.

00:01:52.970 --> 00:01:55.520
After that, we can run
the word count example

00:01:55.520 --> 00:01:58.490
and specify the input
and output files.

00:01:58.490 --> 00:02:00.560
Since we're not
specifying a runner,

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:04.430
the default is to use the direct
runner, which runs the pipeline

00:02:04.430 --> 00:02:05.750
right on your machine.

00:02:05.750 --> 00:02:08.630
If you're just getting
started or looking to debug,

00:02:08.630 --> 00:02:10.639
the direct runner
is likely how you'll

00:02:10.639 --> 00:02:12.260
want to run your pipelines.

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:15.440
After all, Beam is designed so
you can run the same pipeline

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:18.420
code on different runners.

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:21.420
Your local environment might
be fine for "King Lear,"

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:23.160
but what if your
batch data job was

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:26.040
counting all of the words
for an entire library's

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:27.060
worth of books?

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:29.760
In that case, we can just
provide a few more options

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:34.290
to the code and use Google Cloud
Dataflow as the runner instead.

00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:37.320
The input file comes from
a publicly-accessible Cloud

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:40.440
Storage file, while the
output will be a Cloud Storage

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:42.720
bucket that I created earlier.

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:45.000
We're also specifying
to use the Dataflow

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:48.300
runner and some other details,
like which Google Cloud project

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:49.500
this runs on.

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:51.360
Once we run this
command, the job

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:54.280
will be created and Dataflow
and start processing.

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:57.650
Let's take a closer
look at the code.

00:02:57.650 --> 00:03:00.170
The core part of the
code happens here,

00:03:00.170 --> 00:03:02.660
after the pipeline has been
set up with these options

00:03:02.660 --> 00:03:04.520
that we provided.

00:03:04.520 --> 00:03:06.650
This first part of the
pipeline reads the text

00:03:06.650 --> 00:03:10.010
from the input file and creates
a PCollection of each line

00:03:10.010 --> 00:03:11.900
from the file.

00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:15.740
From here, we use read transform
from the line's PCollection.

00:03:15.740 --> 00:03:18.860
The first transform to split
the line into separate words,

00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:22.280
one transform to turn each
word into a single kind of one,

00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:24.470
and another transform
to group and sum

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:26.390
all of the words and counts.

00:03:26.390 --> 00:03:28.550
The pipe operator
for Beam and Python

00:03:28.550 --> 00:03:31.770
is short for applying a
transformation to a collection.

00:03:31.770 --> 00:03:33.590
So with this short
block of code,

00:03:33.590 --> 00:03:36.170
we're able to chain the
PTransforms together

00:03:36.170 --> 00:03:39.560
by using the output PCollection
of the previous transform

00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:41.060
as the input.

00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:44.120
ParDo is a transformation
that takes each element

00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:47.570
from the input PCollection
and performs a function on it.

00:03:47.570 --> 00:03:49.910
In this case, we
have a custom regex

00:03:49.910 --> 00:03:53.030
that simply looks for groups
of non-white space characters

00:03:53.030 --> 00:03:56.490
and splits each line
of text into words.

00:03:56.490 --> 00:04:00.270
The final transform formats the
text using a custom function

00:04:00.270 --> 00:04:02.420
and finally outputs
it to the bucket.

00:04:02.420 --> 00:04:04.530
This is a fairly
straightforward pipeline,

00:04:04.530 --> 00:04:07.830
so these few transforms
are all we need.

00:04:07.830 --> 00:04:10.320
While this is running, let's
take a look at Dataflow

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:11.460
in the Cloud Console.

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:13.200
You can see the
status of the job

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:14.910
in each step of the pipeline.

00:04:14.910 --> 00:04:18.600
The name for each transform
step comes from the code.

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:22.019
You can also click on each
step to see more details.

00:04:22.019 --> 00:04:24.300
Over time, the
interface will update,

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:27.270
and pretty soon, the
pipeline will be complete.

00:04:27.270 --> 00:04:29.070
Beam is designed
to run pipelines

00:04:29.070 --> 00:04:30.520
with parallel processing.

00:04:30.520 --> 00:04:33.810
So if you're working on a large
batch or a streaming data job,

00:04:33.810 --> 00:04:35.880
the pipeline still
looks the same.

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:38.100
Thankfully, Google
Cloud and Dataflow

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:40.870
will handle all the
infrastructure details for us.

00:04:40.870 --> 00:04:43.440
So with just that
short bit of code,

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:46.020
we've been able to get a
count of all the words.

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:49.150
Now, if we take a look
at the output file,

00:04:49.150 --> 00:04:52.740
we can see the count of all
the words in "King Lear."

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:55.650
However, you might notice that
there are two separate counts

00:04:55.650 --> 00:04:57.090
for the word "king."

00:04:57.090 --> 00:04:59.340
That's because we didn't
take capitalization

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:00.960
to account when grouping.

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:02.700
The easiest way
to fix that would

00:05:02.700 --> 00:05:04.980
be to add another PTransform.

00:05:04.980 --> 00:05:07.620
After we split the
sentence up into words,

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:10.320
we could simply
lowercase all the words

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:13.710
and then pass that PCollection
over to the group and sum

00:05:13.710 --> 00:05:15.360
transformation.

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:18.930
Apache Beam is a powerful
tool to help you process data.

00:05:18.930 --> 00:05:21.300
And the word count
example we just went over

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:23.610
is a simple way to get started.

00:05:23.610 --> 00:05:25.440
There's some links
in the documentation

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:28.740
with more information, including
an interactive tutorial you

00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:31.320
can take right in the
Google Cloud Console.

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:32.640
Thanks for watching.

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:35.990
[UPBEAT MUSIC]

